## Why This Is Asked
This question probes end-to-end design for tenant isolation, scalability, and disaster readiness in GCP.

## Key Concepts
- Tenant isolation via per-tenant topics, datasets, and IAM bindings
- Streaming ingestion with Dataflow and Pub/Sub
- Schema registry and drift detection in Cloud Storage
- Private connectivity (PSC) to BigQuery; VPC Service Controls
- Observability, cost-aware ops, and multi-region DR

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-tenant routing helper (illustrative)
function getTenantResources(tenantId){
  const ds = `tenant_${tenantId}_ds`;
  const table = `events_${tenantId}`;
  return { ds, table };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test schema drift and implement rollback?
- How would you monitor per-tenant QoS and cost overruns?

## Why This Is Asked
Rationale: assesses ability to design an end-to-end event-driven pipeline with multi-tenant isolation, idempotence, and basic observability using familiar GCP services.

## Key Concepts
- Event-driven design with Pub/Sub and Cloud Run
- Multi-tenant isolation and per-tenant storage
- Idempotent processing via event_id
- Per-tenant BigQuery partitions; environment separation
- Observability and simple tests

## Code Example
```javascript
function isDuplicate(event, cache) {
  const key = `${event.tenant_id}:${event.event_id}`;
  if (cache.has(key)) return true;
  cache.set(key, true);
  return false;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle schema evolution per tenant while keeping backward compatibility?
- What are the tradeoffs of using Redis vs in-process cache for idempotence?

## Why This Is Asked
